   "q_en": "<p>In a quadrilateral UVWX, all sides are equal and &#8736;XUV = 90&deg;. If UV is produced to a point Y and UW is produced to a point Z, then find the value of &ang;YVW + &ang;ZWV.</p>",
   "q_hi": "<p>&#2319;&#2325; &#2330;&#2340;&#2369;&#2352;&#2381;&#2349;&#2369;&#2332; UVWX &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2349;&#2368; &#2349;&#2369;&#2332;&#2366;&#2319;&#2305; &#2348;&#2352;&#2366;&#2348;&#2352; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306; &#2324;&#2352; &#8736;XUV = 90<sup>o </sup>&#2361;&#2376;&#2404; &#2351;&#2342;&#2367; UV &#2325;&#2379; &#2348;&#2367;&#2306;&#2342;&#2369; Y &#2340;&#2325; &#2348;&#2397;&#2366;&#2351;&#2366; &#2332;&#2366;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376; &#2324;&#2352; UW &#2325;&#2379; &#2348;&#2367;&#2306;&#2342;&#2369; Z &#2340;&#2325; &#2348;&#2397;&#2366;&#2351;&#2366; &#2332;&#2366;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; &#8736;YVW + &#8736;ZWV &#2325;&#2366; &#2350;&#2366;&#2344; &#2332;&#2381;&#2334;&#2366;&#2340; &#2325;&#2368;&#2332;&#2367;&#2319;&#2404;</p>",
   "options_en": [
    "180&deg;",
    "125&deg;",
    "270&deg;",
    "225&deg;"
   ],
   "options_hi": [
    "180<sup>o</sup>",
    "125<sup>o</sup>",
    "270<sup>o</sup>",
    "225<sup>o</sup>"
   ],
   "answer": 3,
   "solution_en": "<p>Since in quadrilateral UVWX, all sides are equal and &#8736;XUV = 90<sup>o</sup>, so UVWX is a square.</p><p>Let the square UVWX looks like this, when UV is produced to a point Y and UW is produced to a point Z:</p><p><img width=316 height=196 id=\"Picture 22\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image023.png\"></p><p>UW is a diagonal (i.e., a bisector), so, &#8736;XUW =&#8736;WUV=&#8736;VWU=&#8736;UWX=90/2 =45<sup>o</sup></p><p>UY is a straight line with angle 180<sup>o</sup>. So, &#8736;YVW=180-90 =90<sup>o</sup></p><p>Similarly, &#8736;VWO = 90<sup>o</sup></p><p>&#8736;OWZ=&#8736;UWX=45<sup>o</sup> (Vertically opposite angles)</p><p>&#8756; <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 59\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >YVW + <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 60\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >ZWV = <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 61\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >YVW + <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 62\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >OWZ+<img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 63\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >VWO</p><p>= 90+90+45 =225<sup>o</sup></p>",
   "solution_hi": "<p>Since in quadrilateral UVWX, all sides are equal and &#8736;XUV = 90<sup>o</sup>, so UVWX is a square.</p><p>Let the square UVWX looks like this, when UV is produced to a point Y and UW is produced to a point Z:</p><p><img width=316 height=196 id=\"Picture 22\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image023.png\"></p><p>UW is a diagonal (i.e., a bisector), so, &#8736;XUW =&#8736;WUV=&#8736;VWU=&#8736;UWX=90/2 =45<sup>o</sup></p><p>UY is a straight line with angle 180<sup>o</sup>. So, &#8736;YVW=180-90 =90<sup>o</sup></p><p>Similarly, &#8736;VWO = 90<sup>o</sup></p><p>&#8736;OWZ=&#8736;UWX=45<sup>o</sup> (Vertically opposite angles)</p><p>&#8756; <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 59\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >YVW + <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 60\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >ZWV = <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 61\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >YVW + <img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 62\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >OWZ+<img width=13 height=8 id=\"Picture 63\" src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p18085/image020.png\" >VWO</p><p>= 90+90+45 =225<sup>o</sup></p>",
   "marks": 1.0,
